                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "TLDR: No, just no.\n\nI grabbed this in a bundle and installed expecting a basic mahjong game with a bit of a story glommed on and maybe some light RPG elements, something like a mahjong version of Regency Solitaire or Chef Solitaire. It is definitely a mahjong game with a bit of story, if you consider the character's proceeding from a monk to a couple more monks to her true love and hearing a couple stanzas of poetry from each a story. She walks from puzzle to puzzle, and while you solve she continually pulls something out of her pocket (presumably the one ring as often as she checks to make sure it's still there) in an animation that it took me a third of the game to realize wasn't her scratching herself. But of course all of that is just window dressing and is unimportant if the mahjong itself is good.\n\nIt's not. It looks and plays like the basic mahjong you can play on any number of free or paid apps on any number of platforms. But it's missing one aspect of those that I consider to be kind of crucial: IT'S NOT POSSIBLE TO LOSE! Without the possibility of failing this is really not so much mahjong as it is just matching things until the game is over. It has a shuffle button that never runs out and will continue to rearrange the tiles until you've cleared them all. Even if you whittle the game down to two matching tiles stacked on each other it will rearrange them so they can be cleared. After about a third of the game I sort of realized this and stopped playing strategically. After about half I started actively trying to make the worst possible matches in order to engineer a loss and see what happened. I failed miserably, the game just won't let you lose. \n\nSo since there's nothing to recommend the \"gameplay\" and there aren't Steam cards I could only recommend this to maybe the parent of a slow child who needs a self-esteem boost. Anyone else would be better served by literally any other mahjong game out there.",
